(D,"mcq",
"16. During UT examination of a CRA (corrosion resistant alloy) clad pressure vessel, the technician is calibrating using a reference block with a notch. The CRA cladding is Alloy 625 overlaid by submerged arc welding on a 40 mm CS base plate. What is the primary challenge for UT inspection of this component?",
"The CRA cladding absorbs most of the ultrasonic beam, preventing penetration into the CS base plate.\nThe acoustic impedance mismatch and coarse grain structure of the Alloy 625 overlay causes high attenuation, beam skewing, and mode conversion, making reliable defect detection in the weld and base metal significantly more difficult.\nCRA cladding requires a different probe frequency (below 1 MHz) to penetrate effectively.\nCRA clad inspections require gamma radiography, not UT, because of the multi-layer interface.",
"The acoustic impedance mismatch and coarse grain structure of the Alloy 625 overlay causes high attenuation, beam skewing, and mode conversion, making reliable defect detection in the weld and base metal significantly more difficult.",
"CRA overlays (especially nickel alloys with coarse austenitic grain structure) present significant UT challenges: high attenuation of beam energy, anisotropic acoustic properties causing beam steering, and mode conversion at the CRA-to-CS interface. Standard calibration blocks for CS do not adequately represent the real material. PAUT with focused low-frequency probes or TOFD on the CS side is often required for reliable examination.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"17. An RT technician performs a double-wall double-image (DWDI) exposure on a 3-inch schedule 40 pipe weld. The source is placed perpendicular to the pipe axis. The film shows both walls of the pipe weld superimposed. When measuring the defect length on the film, what correction must be applied?",
"No correction is needed — the DWDI image is a direct 1:1 projection of the weld.\nA geometric magnification correction factor must be applied, accounting for the source-to-object and object-to-film distances, to determine the actual defect size in the weld.\nThe defect length must be doubled because both pipe walls are superimposed in the image.\nThe defect length must be halved because the DWDI technique captures two walls simultaneously.",
"A geometric magnification correction factor must be applied, accounting for the source-to-object and object-to-film distances, to determine the actual defect size in the weld.",
"In DWDI techniques, the image is magnified due to the geometric relationship between the source, object, and film. The actual defect size = measured film size / magnification factor (SFD/(SFD-t), where t is the material thickness to the defect). Not applying this correction leads to overestimating defect sizes.",
1),

